# rc.d configuration for the Polytropos services.
#
# Installed as /usr/local/etc/polytropos.conf and sourced by both rc.d
# scripts. Every setting uses the `: ${var:="value"}` form, which assigns
# only when the variable is unset — so the precedence is:
#
#     /etc/rc.conf   (highest — rc.subr reads it before this file)
#     this file
#     the rc.d script's own defaults (lowest)
#
# --- this file is small on purpose ------------------------------------
#
# It holds only what rc needs *before any Python runs*: which command to
# exec, which account to drop to, where to write logs. Everything the
# tool reads for itself — the queue root, the state database, the listen
# address, the models, the attempt limits, the policy, delivery — lives
# in one settings file instead:
#
#     /usr/local/etc/polytropos/polytropos.toml
#
#     dportsv3 config show     every value, and where it came from
#     dportsv3 config check    validate without starting anything
#
# It used to be otherwise. This file declared polytropos_state_db and the
# rc script translated it into $DPORTSV3_STATE_DB, and the same for the
# artifact root, the tracker URL, the bind address, the port and the
# default dev-env — one setting with two names in two formats, kept in
# step by hand. Two more shell files, harness.env and chat.env, existed
# only so `export` could carry values into os.environ, which was the one
# channel a setting had into the code. None of that is needed now.
#
# Credentials are not here and never were: this file is world-readable.
# They live one per file under /usr/local/etc/polytropos/secrets/, so
# each mode can follow whichever service reads it.

# The commands the services run. Not a repository path: these are two
# entry points, and where they come from is nobody's business but the
# operator's. A packaged install has both in the prefix bindir.
#
# From a git checkout, point them at the wrapper instead:
#
#     : ${polytropos_cmd:="/home/you/polytropos/bin/dportsv3"}
#     : ${polytropos_dev_env_cmd:="/home/you/polytropos/bin/dportsv3 dev-env"}
#
# The wrapper is the entry point that knows the repository layout — it
# exports DPORTSV3_CONFIG_DIR, DPORTS_DEV_TOOL_ROOT and DPORTSV3_CMD for
# everything downstream, and it builds the venv on demand. The packaged
# console scripts need none of that.
: ${polytropos_cmd:="/usr/local/bin/dportsv3"}

# Only the runner uses this one: it is how the agent reaches a chroot.
# The `dportsv3` console script deliberately does NOT implement `dev-env`,
# so this must be dports-dev-env (or a checkout wrapper plus that word).
: ${polytropos_dev_env_cmd:="/usr/local/bin/dports-dev-env"}

# PATH for the services. rc.subr's own PATH leaves out the local prefix,
# which is where python3 and dports-dev-env live on DragonFly — without
# this the checkout wrapper cannot find an interpreter at all.
: ${polytropos_path:="/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin"}

# The unprivileged account that owns the evidence tree and runs the
# tracker. The queue runner ignores this and runs as root, because
# dev-env requires it.
: ${polytropos_user:="polytropos"}
: ${polytropos_group:="polytropos"}

# HOME for the service that drops privileges. `daemon -u` lowers the uid
# but leaves HOME inherited from root, so it has to be said here or the
# tracker runs with HOME=/root and cannot read it.
: ${polytropos_home:="/var/db/polytropos"}

# Where the settings file and the secrets directory live. Both services
# export this as $DPORTSV3_CONFIG_DIR, which is the only way anything in
# the Python packages finds a config file — nothing searches for a
# surrounding directory. It cannot itself be a setting, for the obvious
# reason that it is what names the settings file.
: ${polytropos_config_dir:="/usr/local/etc/polytropos"}

# Service output. daemon(8) appends each service's stdout and stderr
# here; newsyslog rotates it through the drop-in this install placed in
# /usr/local/etc/newsyslog.conf.d/.
: ${polytropos_log_dir:="/var/log/polytropos"}
